  "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: The phrase \"soft power\" appears in no document of the series, so the question is read as one about information programs, psychological operations and educational and cultural exchange. On that reading FRUS answers it well for 1949 to 1978. Officials treated persuasion as a companion to aid and arms, aimed at peoples they thought poorly informed about America. From the 1950s they preferred to work through local governments and keep Americans out of sight. Their own verdicts were sober: the Information Agency's director called information and psychological work the weakest part of the war effort in Vietnam, an adviser argued that deeds outweighed broadcasts, and by 1975 a field post judged security programs to lack credibility. Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed documents came from the Information Agency's director's and plans files (Record Group 306), State Department central files and Operations Coordinating Board files (Record Group 59) and the Johnson Library's National Security File; the footnotes cite without printing, most often, the State Department's National Security Council policy-paper records (NARA identifier 2839192), the Johnson Library's National Security File, the Nixon National Security Council files and the Kennedy Library's National Security Files. What this request cannot get from FRUS: audiences' own reactions beyond a few quoted polls, the broadcasts and publications themselves, the Agency's research reports (look in its record group), and any systematic record of the exchange programs for Japan, Korea and the Philippines. \"Soft power\" itself was searched across the whole series and found nowhere.",
